@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Self-Hosting";
}

<div class="editorial">
    <header class="editorial-head">
        <p class="metro-kicker">run your own</p>
        <h1 class="metro-title metro-title--compact">How to Self-Host Ohrbob</h1>
        <p class="editorial-lede">Ohrbob is open-source and designed to be easily self-hostable. Keep total control of your data and run your own private podcast sync server.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="editorial-section">
        <h2 class="editorial-section__title">1. Architecture Overview</h2>
        <p>The Ohrbob ecosystem consists of several interconnected pieces:</p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
            <li><strong>Ohrbob.Api:</strong> The backend REST API built in .NET, managing users, stations, feeds, and the sync state.</li>
            <li><strong>Ohrbob.Web:</strong> The frontend web UI built with HTMX and Razor Pages.</li>
            <li><strong>Ohrbob.Player:</strong> The playback API handling media streams and playback configuration.</li>
            <li><strong>MongoDB:</strong> The primary data store for all user data, station configurations, and playhead states.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section">
        <h2 class="editorial-section__title">2. Deploying via Docker Compose (Recommended)</h2>
        <p>The easiest way to get started is by using Docker Compose. We provide an all-in-one <code>docker-compose.yml</code> file in our repository.</p>
        <pre class="editorial-code"><code>git clone https://github.com/ohrbob-org/ohrbob.server.git
cd ohrbob.server
cp .env-example .env
# Edit .env with your desired secrets and configuration
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section">
        <h2 class="editorial-section__title">3. Configuration &amp; Secrets</h2>
        <p>Ohrbob requires a few environment variables to operate securely. You should configure these in your <code>.env</code> file:</p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
            <li><code>ApiKeys__0__Key</code> &amp; <code>OhrbobApi__ApiKey</code>: A matching secret string used to authenticate the Web UI with the REST API.</li>
            <li><code>PodcastIndex__ApiKey</code> &amp; <code>PodcastIndex__ApiSecret</code>: Credentials for the PodcastIndex API (required for search and discovery). You can get these for free at PodcastIndex.org.</li>
            <li><code>MongoDbData__ConnectionString</code>: Automatically configured if using the provided Docker Compose stack, but can be pointed to an external instance.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section">
        <h2 class="editorial-section__title">4. Authentication Providers</h2>
        <p>You can enable OpenID Connect (OIDC) providers like Google, GitHub, or your own Keycloak instance by adding them to the configuration, allowing seamless single sign-on for your users.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section">
        <h2 class="editorial-section__title">5. Client Connections</h2>
        <p>Once your server is running, you can connect the Avalonia desktop/mobile app by navigating to the Settings screen in the app, entering your self-hosted server URL, and logging in using the API Key generated in your web profile.</p>
    </section>
</div>
